      "text": "A bicycle operates through a harmonious integration of mechanical leverage, rotational motion, and human biomechanics, where the rider\u2019s muscular effort is converted into forward propulsion via a chain-driven transmission system. When the rider pedals, the force applied to the crank arms rotates the chainring, which pulls the chain to turn the rear cog, thereby transferring torque to the rear wheel hub; this rotational energy is then transformed into linear motion through the frictional grip between the tire and the ground, allowing the bicycle to move forward while maintaining stability through gyroscopic effects and the castering action of the front fork. Simultaneously, the rider\u2019s weight distribution and steering inputs control the direction and balance, while the frame\u2019s geometry ensures structural integrity, creating a dynamic system where human energy is efficiently amplified by mechanical advantage to overcome air resistance and rolling friction.<|im_end|>",
