      "text": "A suspension bridge carries its loads through a sophisticated system of tension-based force distribution, where the primary structural elements work in concert to transfer weight from the deck to the ground. The process begins with the vertical suspender cables, which hang from the main suspension cables and directly support the bridge deck, transferring the live and dead loads upward to the main cables. These main cables, draped over the tops of the two tall towers, are the critical load-bearing components; they function primarily in pure tension, pulling downward on the towers and outward toward the anchorages. The towers, in turn, convert this horizontal tension into vertical compression, transferring the load down to their foundations, while the massive anchorages at either end of the bridge resist the horizontal pull of the main cables, preventing them from sliding or deforming. This entire system relies on the high tensile strength of steel cables, allowing the bridge to span vast distances with minimal material usage, as the forces are efficiently ch",
