      "text": "Fiber-optic communication transmits information by converting electrical signals into light pulses, typically generated by a laser or LED, which are then guided through a thin, flexible strand of glass or plastic fiber. This transmission relies on the principle of total internal reflection, where the light is confined within the fiber's core by a surrounding cladding layer with a lower refractive index, ensuring that the light bounces off the core-cladding interface and travels long distances with minimal signal loss or attenuation. As the light pulses propagate, they carry data encoded as binary information (on/off states or varying intensities), and upon reaching the receiving end, a photodetector converts these optical signals back into electrical signals for processing by electronic devices, enabling high-bandwidth, low-latency, and interference-resistant communication over vast distances.<|im_end|>",
